deployment.yaml
Core file
Defines how Kubernetes runs your application, including which container image to use and how many replicas to maintain.
Think of it as the application manager.




service.yaml
Gives your application a stable network endpoint so other applications, or sometimes external users, can reach its Pods.
Think of it as the network access point.




configmap.yaml
Stores non-sensitive configuration, such as application settings or environment-specific values.
Think of it as the settings file for your application.
Don't store passwords or API tokens here.




secret.yaml
Defines Kubernetes Secret resources for sensitive data, such as passwords and tokens.
Think of it as a credential container. Base64-encoded values are not encrypted by themselves, so don't commit real secrets to a public repository.




namespace.yaml
Creates a Kubernetes Namespace, which separates resources into logical groups within a cluster.
Think of it as a workspace or compartment.




hpa.yaml
Defines a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA), which can increase or decrease the number of application Pods based on configured metrics and limits.
Think of it as automatic capacity adjustment. It needs suitable metrics and a compatible workload.




ingress.yaml
Defines rules for routing external HTTP/HTTPS traffic to services inside the cluster.
Think of it as a web traffic gateway. It generally requires an Ingress controller to implement the rules.




pod.yaml
Defines a Pod directly. A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes and can contain one or more containers.
Think of it as a running application unit. In most real deployments, you manage Pods through Deployments instead.




newdeployment.yaml
Likely contains an alternative or newer Deployment configuration. The exact purpose depends on its contents; Kubernetes does not give this filename a special meaning.




multiplenamespace.yaml
Likely defines multiple Namespace resources in one file. The actual contents determine which namespaces it creates.




prodnamespace.yaml
Likely creates a Namespace intended for production resources. The filename is just a label; the manifest determines the actual namespace name and configuration.
